<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis-Joseph Papineau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis-Joseph Papineau was a lawyer and leader of the Parti canadien, later the Parti patriote, in Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and speaker of its assembly for most of 1815 to 1837. He demanded control of revenues and an elected legislative council, set out in the Ninety-Two Resolutions of 1834. When rebellion broke out in 1837 he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, returning after an amnesty in 1845.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-papineau.jpg" alt="Louis-Joseph Papineau" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Notman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis-Joseph_Papineau_by_William_Notman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
